# Finite integral lattice for algebraic cohomology evaluations

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.evaluation_lattice` (`21fc32a6-cbbe-4747-8ec6-4c49da170bd3`), by
davidloeffler, 2026-09-06.

Proof: submission `c1f75f61-2369-4099-8e5d-db2cfee0025a`, by allychan327, 2026-09-06 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

For two algebraic cohomology classes, all coefficient evaluations on [∞]−[r], divided by
binomial(n,j) for 0≤j≤n, lie in a finitely generated integral lattice. The varying rational cusps
introduce no denominators into integral coefficient evaluation. Base change expresses each class as
a finite algebraic linear combination of integral classes, and there are only finitely many nonzero
binomial denominators.

## Explanation of the source proof

Formal argument, taking the two hard inputs as hypotheses.

Write `e` for the base-change isomorphism `Qbar ⊗_Z Hc(N,n,Z) ≃ Hc(N,n,Qbar)` supplied
by `hQ`.  Each class `ψ s` is `e` applied to some tensor, and every element of a tensor
product is a *finite* sum of pure tensors, so

    ψ s = Σ_{i ∈ S s} c_i • e (1 ⊗ φ_i),   c_i ∈ Qbar,  φ_i ∈ Hc(N,n,Z),

using `c ⊗ φ = c • (1 ⊗ φ)` for the left Qbar-action.

The pure-tensor clause of `BaseChange` says `e (1 ⊗ φ)` extends `φ` coefficientwise.
Since `evaluation j r` is the `X^j Y^{n-j}`-coefficient at `[∞] − [r]`, and
`coeff m (map ι P) = ι (coeff m P)`, we get

    evaluation j r (e (1 ⊗ φ)) = (evaluation j r φ : Z) in Qbar,

i.e. the evaluations of the base-changed integral classes are *integers*.  This is the
point where the varying rational cusp `r` is harmless: `r` moves the integer, never the
denominator.

By Qbar-linearity of `evaluation j r`,

    evaluation j r (ψ s) = Σ_i c_i · m_{i,j,r},   m_{i,j,r} ∈ Z,

hence

    evaluation j r (ψ s) / C(n,j) = Σ_i m_{i,j,r} • (c_i / C(n,j)),

a Z-linear combination of the finite set

    G = { c_i / C(n,j) : i ∈ S true ∪ S false, 0 ≤ j ≤ n }.

So the whole evaluation set lies in `span_Z G`, and a Z-submodule of a finitely
generated Z-module is finitely generated because Z is Noetherian
(`Submodule.FG.of_le`).

Note the binomials never vanish for `j ≤ n`, but the proof does not need that: division
is by an arbitrary scalar and the identity `a·m/C = m • (a/C)` holds in any field.

